  "question": {
    "text": "In the circuit shown in the figure. <math><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is a variable resistance<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/75192_CA.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 127px;\" /><br />\r<br />\rAs the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math> is changed, current <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi></math> though the cell varies as shown. Obviously, the variation is asymptotic, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&nbsp;</mi><mn>6</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>→</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∞</mi></math> . Resistances <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> are, respectively",
    "image": null
  },
